---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: "Are the images saved at full quality?", a: "Yes. Each picture is saved at the resolution stored in the PDF, not the smaller size it is shown at on the page." },
  { q: "Why are no images found?", a: "The PDF may contain only text and drawn shapes, which are not images. Use PDF to JPG to save whole pages as pictures instead." },
  { q: "Why do I get one big image per page?", a: "That PDF is a scan. A scanner stores each page as one picture, so that is what gets saved." },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="save-images-from-pdf" faqs={faqs}>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/extract-images-from-pdf">Extract Images</a> and select the PDF. Every page is scanned for pictures automatically. Save the ones you want, or click <strong>Download all</strong> for a .zip. Photos come out as JPG and images with transparency as PNG.</p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/extract-images-from-pdf">pdfsherpa.com/extract-images-from-pdf</a>.</li>
    <li>Select or drop your PDF. Progress shows as each page is checked.</li>
    <li>Browse the gallery. Each image shows its page number and size in pixels.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Save</strong> on an image, or <strong>Download all</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Why not just take a screenshot?</h2>
  <p>
    A screenshot captures the picture at your screen’s size, often much smaller than the original, and includes anything drawn on top of it.
    Extracting saves the actual image file stored inside the PDF, usually at a much higher resolution.
  </p>

  <h2>Good to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A logo repeated on every page is saved once, not fifty times.</li>
    <li>Tiny graphics under 24 pixels, like icons, are skipped.</li>
    <li>Charts drawn with lines and shapes are not images. Capture them with <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a>, then crop with <a href="/crop-image">Crop Image</a>.</li>
    <li>Large images can be made smaller with <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use images responsibly</h2>
  <p>Only reuse pictures you own or have permission to use. Product photos, book illustrations and stock images are usually protected by copyright.</p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>Images are read by pdf.js inside your browser. Your PDF is never uploaded.</p>
</GuideLayout>
